cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework and common state
  representation for describing how a shared multidimensional real-resource
  world K, actor-specific access and usability views K_i, and actor-specific
  subjective evaluation and expectation states P_i condition actor-side
  processes and events A_i. K_i is not required to be a set-theoretic subset
  of K; it is a derived actor-relative access, usability, exercisability, or
  referenceability view of the shared resource world. For another actor a,
  P_j(a) denotes actor j's evaluation, belief, credit assessment, or expectation
  about a, rather than a state held by a. Delta K(S,tau) is reserved for a
  specification-indexed state or resource change descriptor. In general,
  Y_S(t)=M_S(K_t) and Delta K(S,tau)=delta_S(Y_S(t0),Y_S(t1)) in D_S, where the
  change operator need not be an arithmetic difference. Gross production,
  consumption, transaction volume, transformation, and other interval activity
  statistics are not folded into Delta K; when needed they are represented by
  projection-local derived path functionals such as
  H_S(tau)=h_S((K_t)_{t in tau},A_tau). A_tau contains interval actor-side
  events or processes and may retain event ordering where required. Where an
  expectation is defined, a quantitative representation q_S:D_S->V_S is
  specified and E_i[Delta K(S,tau)] is shorthand for the expectation of
  q_S(Delta K(S,tau)). Delta P_i is likewise a change descriptor or state
  transition and need not be an arithmetic difference. Planned or chosen
  resource changes are distinct from forecasts and may be represented by
  projection-local variables such as x_i. Micro and macro observations refer
  to the same common K and K transitions under different observation or
  accounting specifications S; a universal micro-to-macro aggregation map is
  not required by the Core. Economic and social projections may additionally
  test three functional optimization hypotheses: resource-realization through
  action choice, sustainable activity-flow maximization through K/P
  reconfiguration, and downside minimization of distributed P through resource
  and action allocation. These are functions rather than ontological actor
  types: a single autarkic actor may perform all three, while capitalist
  institutions may distribute them primarily across individuals, firms, and
  the state. The concrete objective functionals are projection-specific rather
  than Core primitives. Economic budget feasibility, inter-agent compatibility,
  market-equilibrium conditions, accounting identities, surplus,
  decentralized adjustment, and reputation- or credit-economy models remain
  domain-specific projection candidates rather than universal consequences of
  the VFT Core. VFT does not fix a universal probability measure,
  expectation-formation equation, action-selection rule, state-transition law,
  compatibility condition, market-equilibrium condition, optimization
  objective, or aggregation law.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
